AxiomOS / Justicar

An operating system where the ethics are the infrastructure.

AxiomOS is a constitutional AI system: governance, privacy, and trust are enforced at the boundary every action must cross — not bolted on at the application layer. Most systems treat ethics as policy: rules that can be edited away or routed around. AxiomOS treats them as structure — constraints every action passes through by construction.

The constitutional core is implemented and running on local, consumer hardware. This repo holds the working code and the architecture it's growing into.

What works today

A working governance stack, running locally on open models (via Ollama) — no cloud dependency, nothing leaves the machine unless an action is explicitly and constitutionally permitted to send it:

Constitutional gate — every action an agent attempts is judged against the constitution before it runs. A deterministic rule layer hard-blocks known-dangerous operations; a model layer judges the ambiguous ones. It fails closed: if a decision can't be reached, the action is denied, not allowed. Reads are confined to allowed folders, writes to the project, credential files (SSH keys, .env, etc.) are refused in both directions, and commands that upload local data are blocked. Its guarantees are frozen in a regression suite (core/gate_tests.py).
Tamper-evident ledger — every decision (allowed or blocked, with its reasoning) is written to a hash-chained, append-only audit log. Any edit, deletion, or reorder of past entries is detectable by verifying the chain. Writers are serialized with a file lock, an anchor file plus a hash you keep off the machine catches a full rewrite or truncation, and a watchdog (core/ledger_watchdog.py) re-checks the chain on a timer.
Persistent memory / knowledge graph — a local SQLite knowledge graph (entities + typed relations) with full-text recall, so the system accumulates and queries what it has learned across sessions instead of starting cold each time.
Governed agentic loop — a natural-language agent that acts through tools, where every tool call is routed through the gate and logged. There is no unobserved action.
Scope / authorization enforcement — actions against external targets (bug-bounty recon) are checked against the program's official scope before execution; anything out of scope, or whose scope can't be verified, is refused at the gate. The bounty module itself is optional and not included in this repo; without it, scoped actions are always refused.
Mastery Engine (prototype) — an adaptive Python tutor: 33 concepts in three difficulty tiers, spaced repetition (Leitner boxes), model-written questions graded against a fixed ground-truth brief, and code challenges where pass/fail is decided by actually running your code, not by the model.
Running it

Requires Python 3.11+, Linux (or macOS), and Ollama with the models named in src/core/config.py pulled. Install the Python packages with:

pip install requests ddgs       # ddgs is optional; it enables web_search
sudo apt install firejail       # sandbox for generated code (required for run_python / write_and_test)

Run everything from the src/ folder:

cd src
python3 -m core.gate_tests                  # the gate's regression suite (no model needed)
python3 -m core.gate_tests --llm            # also exercise the live gate model
python3 -m core.ledger_watchdog             # verify the ledger chain once
python3 -m capabilities.axiom_chat          # the governed agent (every tool call gated + logged)
python3 mastery/tutor.py                    # the Mastery Engine tutor

Paths default to your home folder. To narrow them, set AXIOM_PROJECT_ROOT (where writes are allowed) and AXIOM_READABLE_ROOTS (colon-separated folders the agent may read). Your learner profile and practice code are saved next to the tutor and are git-ignored.

Installing AxiomOS on a machine or VM

On a fresh Ubuntu or Debian install (test it in a VM first):

sudo apt install -y git
git clone https://github.com/Phenarax-ui/AxiomOS-Justicar.git
cd AxiomOS-Justicar
sudo ./deploy/install.sh

This creates an 'axiom' user whose login shell is the governed AI shell, installs the code read-only under /opt/axiomos (so the AI cannot edit its own gate or constitution), keeps the ledger in /var/lib/axiomos with a root-owned anchor, and checks the ledger every minute. If Ollama runs on another machine (for example the VM's host), pass its address: sudo ./deploy/install.sh http://192.168.122.1:11434

The legacy/ folder holds the first standalone versions of the gate, ledger, memory and agent.

The problem

Current AI infrastructure is centralized, extractive, and ungovernable by the people it affects. Privacy lives in policies that can change overnight. Educational tools surveil the learners they claim to serve. Platforms optimize for engagement over wellbeing. Governance gets captured by whoever has the most money. And underserved communities have no real access to any of it.

AxiomOS addresses these at the architectural level — not with better promises, but with structural constraints that can't be quietly removed.

Why OS-level

Application-layer governance is a bouncer outside a club: anyone who doesn't want to follow the rules uses a different door. AxiomOS puts enforcement in the operating system, in layers: the kernel enforces exact limits on every program (files, network, processes); the constitutional gate judges the meaning of significant actions and fails closed; attestation lets the network detect any node that has removed the constitution; and a signed, witnessed ledger makes rewriting history detectable. The owner of a machine can always modify it — the point is that no application can route around the rules, and no node can drop them without the network noticing. See "Why the kernel" for what each layer does and does not guarantee.

The architecture

Six components, in dependency order:

Component	What it does	Status
Constitutional Layer	The rules everything inherits; enforcement at the boundary.	Implemented
Natural Language Shell	Plain English as the primary interface — describe what you want.	Working prototype
Dual Data Lake	Separated public lake and test (sandbox) lake; the test lake is gated by a hardware key.	Specified
Agora	Federated deliberation and governance; discovery over engagement.	Specified
Mastery Engine	Adaptive education that serves the learner and is barred from surveilling them.	Working prototype (app layer)
Axiom Coin	Non-transferable contribution tracking; a trust cap that blocks governance capture by money.	Specified

Designed to run over an 802.11ah (HaLow) mesh so it doesn't depend on centralized internet infrastructure. Detailed design notes live alongside this README (kernel architecture, extended architecture, mesh layer, constitution draft).

Status & roadmap

The constitutional core — gate, audit ledger, memory, and governed execution — is implemented and running on consumer hardware. The remaining components are specified and on the roadmap.

Phase 1 — Proof of concept: the governance core (working) plus the Mastery Engine as the first full application.
Phase 2 — Network scale.
Phase 3 — Global distribution.

Built by a self-taught systems architect, from the architecture up.

